organ toxicity per year, which results in over
50,000 fatalities and ranks as the second leading
cause of mortality in the United States (KnisleyHenry, 2020). Food poisons are responsible for
5.3% of the 30,400 organ toxicity deaths in
Nigeria that occur in men and 3.8% in women.
However, the peak age remains about 44 years,
and the male-to-female ratio is roughly equal.
The major challenge lies in screening for and
degrading food contaminants, as there is a
dearth of information on the degradation and
detoxification of contaminants, particularly
aflatoxin, heavy metals, and pesticide residues.
Food contamination is a critical concern, as high
toxin levels pose significant health risks.
Protecting the public from the extent of harm
caused by contaminants in food has become very
imperative. There is, however, a dearth of
information on the impact of detoxification
treatment on eliminating or reducing food
contaminants to the barest minimum. Hence,
this research aimed to investigate the potential
of
detoxification
treatment
for
food
contaminants (such as aflatoxins, pesticide
residues, and heavy metals) to proffer a viable
solution to household food contamination, such
as catfish.
MATERIALS AND METHODS
Sample collection
A total of 20 kg of dried catfish, potentially
contaminated, was procured from various
vendors at Bodija market in Ibadan, Nigeria
(7.420 N, 3.900 E). The samples were stored in
sterilized, contaminant-free zip-lock bags and
subsequently kept in a freezer for analysis. The
vinegar and sodium chloride (NaCl) were
purchased from Orchid Medical and Laboratory
Supplies, Jericho, Ibadan (7.39630 N, 3.86440 E).
Corns-steep liquor (acetic acid) was obtained
from sieved fermented corn.
Development of chemical detoxification
For the development of the chemical
detoxification protocol, brine, acetic acid, and
citric acid solutions were used, following the
methodology described by Jubeen et al. (2020).
The brine solution was prepared by dissolving 0.9
g NaCl in 100 mL of distilled water. A 1% acetic
acid solution was prepared by diluting 1 mL of
acetic acid in 100 mL of distilled water.
Development of biological detoxification
Biological degradation of the food contaminant
aflatoxin was conducted using lactic acid
UMYU Journal of Microbiology Research

bacteria and yeast-rich corn steep liquor at
elevated temperatures. To obtain corn-steep
liquor, 3 kg of corn was soaked in water and
fermented naturally for 3 days. Following
fermentation, the resulting liquor was decanted
and stored under refrigeration for use.
Extraction of samples
The catfish were carefully crushed with a mortar
and pestle to expose the inner portions. A 50gram portion of the crushed sample was weighed
using a weighing balance and then tied in filter
paper, preparatory to extraction. The samples
were reflux-extracted in a Soxhlet extractor.
Thereafter, the solvent was removed using a
rotary evaporator, leaving only the extracted
oil. The solid residue, which is the non-soluble
portion of the extract, was discarded.
Aflatoxin determination
A dilute and shoot multi-mycotoxin LC-MS/MS
method was used to determine 11 mycotoxins
regulated in cereals as specified by the EU
legislation 2023/915 for AFB1, AFB2, AFG1,
AFG2, DON, FB, FB2, ZEA, T-2, HT-2, and OTA.
The method of Sulyok et al. (2020), with slight
modifications, was adopted for sample
preparation. Briefly, 5 g of the sample was
weighed into centrifuge tubes and extracted
using a solvent mixture of 79/20/1 (v/v/v)
acetonitrile/ultrapure water/formic acid on a
mechanical shaker for 90 min. An aliquot of the
raw extract was subsequently diluted with
20/79/1
(v/v/v)
acetonitrile/ultrapure
water/formic acid, filtered through a 0.22 µm
nylon filter, and injected into a LC-MS/MS
system without further cleanup. Waters Acquity
H-class UPLC system (Waters, Milford, MA, USA)
was employed to perform chromatographic
separation of selected mycotoxins by using a HSS
T3 column (100 × 2.1 mm, 1.8 µm particle size)
(Waters, Milford, MA, USA) at 40 0C. Gradient
elution was carried out with eluent A consisting
of an aqueous solution of ammonium formate (5
mM) and eluent B being methanol at a constant
flow rate of 0.3 mL/min. The injected sample
volume was 10 µL. The separation started with
95% eluent A, followed by linearly decreasing it
to 50% eluent A in 6 min and in the next 4 min to
5% eluent A with a hold time of 5 min, afterward
switching to 95% eluent A and column
equilibration to initial conditions in the next 3
min. The UPLC system was coupled to a Waters
Xevo TQD tandem mass spectrometer (Waters,
Milford, MA, USA) equipped with an electrospray
ionization (ESI) interface.

