Mitigation of Aflatoxin, Heavy Metals and Pesticide Residues in Dried Catfish
using Chemical and Biological Treatments
1,2*Ogungbemi, K., 1Nwikwe, D. C., 2Solomon-Ibuwunwa, O. M., 3Abel, O. O., 1,2Popoola,

S. T., 4Olabisi, B. O.
1

Department of Biochemistry, College of Bioscience, Federal University of Agriculture, Abeokuta, Ogun State, Nigeria
2
Chemistry/Biochemistry Unit, Nigerian Stored Products Research Institute, Ibadan Zonal Office, Nigeria
3
Microbiology Unit, Nigerian Stored Products Research Institute, Ibadan Zonal Office, Nigeria
4
Department of Food Science and Technology, Bowen University, Iwo, Osun State, Nigeria
*Correspondence author: ogungbemikunle@funaab.edu.ng

Abstract
Food contamination poses a worldwide health challenge, with about 600 million cases and
420,000 deaths each year. This study evaluated the effectiveness of selected chemical and
biological post-harvest treatments in reducing contaminants in household consumables,
specifically dried catfish. Twenty kilograms of dried catfish, potentially contaminated,
were obtained from vendors at Bodija market in Ibadan. The samples were homogenized,
and aflatoxin, pesticide residues, and heavy metals were determined using standard
analytical methods before and after treatment. Detoxification/degradation treatments
included brine, acidification with vinegar rich in acetic acid, and lactic acid–rich corn-steep
liquor. All treatments significantly (p<0.05) reduced the concentrations of arsenic,
cadmium, copper, and lead compared with the untreated control. Vinegar achieved the
highest reduction in heavy metals, followed by lactic acid–rich corn-steep liquor, whereas
brine produced only modest decreases and slightly increased iron levels compared to
untreated. All treatments also lowered pesticide residues. Vinegar consistently produced
the largest reductions in DDT, Endosulfan, Aldrin, Lindane, and Heptachlor epoxide,
followed by lactic acid–rich corn-steep liquor, while brine solution produced the least effect.
For aflatoxin (B1, B2, G1, and G2), all treatments significantly reduced aflatoxin levels
compared with the untreated. However, lactic acid–rich corn-steep liquor produced the
greatest decreases, resulting in the lowest aflatoxin concentrations, while vinegar caused
moderate reductions, and brine was least effective. Therefore, acidic treatments,
particularly lactic acid–rich corn-steep liquor and vinegar, produced the most pronounced
detoxifying effects, indicating that organic acid–based solutions are more effective than
saline treatment for improving the safety of dried catfish.
Key words: Aflatoxin, Pesticide residue, Heavy metals, Degradation, Detoxification
INTRODUCTION
The safety and quality of agricultural produce
and food products are of great importance, yet
they are being compromised by various
contaminants entering at any point in the food
chain, including production, storage, processing,
and transportation (Lebelo et al., 2021). One of
the major threats to food safety is the treatment
of agricultural produce or products with
protective agents that may leave contaminants
in products of plant and animal origin (Onyeaka
et al., 2024).
The presence of these
contaminants at concentrations higher than
those considered safe has been implicated in
several disease conditions, such as cancer (Munir
et al., 2021).
Another food contaminant
inherent in food called natural toxins, such as
aflatoxin, may be biosynthesized in foodstuffs
due to wrong processing or storage procedures
UMYU Journal of Microbiology Research

(Samuel et al., 2021), while other contaminants,
such as heavy metals, can be introduced through
water, air, and soil, usually during the value
chain (Singh et al., 2020). When assessing the
risks associated with stock-catfish consumption,
aflatoxin, pesticide residues, and heavy metals
must be regularly monitored from the field to
end users of the products, and their interactions
must be taken into account.
According to Abebe et al. (2020), about 30% of
the total population in industrialized countries
suffers from various health issues annually due
to the consumption of food contaminants. The
World Health Organization (WHO) estimated in
2009 that over 200,000 people die of food
contamination in Nigeria each year (Adebayo,
2023). Additionally, food contamination has
been related to approximately 140,000 cases of

