Pesticide residue determination
Pesticide
residue
determination
for
organochlorides (e.g., Aldrin, DDT, Endosulfan,
Lindane, and Heptachlor epoxide) was carried
out using gas-liquid chromatographic analysis
described by Olulakin et al. (2015). The Soxhletextracted sample was analyzed by gas
chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS).
Model GC94FID operated at these conditions: FID
column 12 ft x 2 mm I.D with OV17 on 100/120
H.P. chromosorb G: N2 at 30 cc/ min, injector
190°C, column, 180°C Detector 200°C.
Standards for all pesticides detected were
prepared at 100 µL/mL using a 10 µL Hamilton
microsyringe; that is, 10 ppm of appropriate
standards was first injected into the GC to
establish their retention times. Identification of
the unknown sample was based on comparing
the retention time of the unknown component
with that of a known standard compound
analyzed under identical conditions.
Heavy metal concentrations
To determine the heavy metals, that is, arsenic
(As), cadmium (Cd), copper (Cu), iron (Fe), and
lead (Pb), the samples were digested and poured
into digestion tubes. The samples were put in a
water bath at 100 0C and boiled for 2 h until all
the tissues were dissolved. The digests were
allowed to cool, then filtered and transferred to
25 mL volumetric flasks, made up to mark with
1% nitric acid. They were kept in plastic bottles
for analysis. The heavy metal concentrations in
the resultant samples were determined using a
flame atomic absorption spectrophotometer

(Model AA-6300, Shimadzu, Japan). All samples
were analyzed in triplicate at 1 mg/kg.
Statistical analysis
All data were statistically expressed as means ±
SEM in three replicates using one-way ANOVA
and compared by Duncan’s post-hoc multiple
comparisons, using IBM SPSS Statistics for
Windows, version 22.0. Values were considered
different statistically at p<0.05
RESULT
Effect of different detoxification treatments
on heavy metal concentrations in dried catfish
Table 1 shows the concentrations of various
heavy metals (As, Cd, Cu, Fe, and Pb) in the
dried catfish samples before and after three
different detoxification treatments. There was
a reduction in As, Cd, Cu, and Pb in the samples
treated with all three solutions when compared
to the untreated sample. Among the three
different treatments, vinegar-rich acetic acid
showed a better detoxification potential for As,
Cd, and Pb when compared to lactic acid-rich
corn-steep liquor and brine solution (e.g., Pb
was reduced from 1.46 ± 0.05 µg/g (Untreated)
to 0.97 ± 0.03 µg/g in vinegar).
The
concentration of Fe increased in the sample
treated with brine solution (from 29.28 ± 2.63
µg/g to 30.57 ± 3.14 µg/g) when compared to
the untreated sample. The vinegar-rich acetic
acid treatment was the most effective in
reducing Fe, decreasing its concentration to the
lowest level of 22.16 ± 2.41 µg/g.

