DISCUSSION
Determination of heavy metals (As, Cd, Cu, Fe,
and
Pb)
showed
treatment-dependent
reductions.
The vinegar (acetic acid-rich)
treatment consistently produced the greatest
reduction in most metals (As, Cd, Cu, Fe, Pb),
with particularly marked removal of Cd and Pb
(Table 1). Researchers have reported that
acidification can promote metal mobilization
and chelation by protonating binding sites and
increasing the solubility of metal–ligand
complexes, a mechanism widely exploited in
chemical leaching and soil/solid washing for Cd,
Pb, and other metals (Rebello et al., 2021;
UMYU Journal of Microbiology Research

Jeyakumar et al., 2022).
In contrast, Fe
concentrations increased after brining, which
may
reflect
salt-enhanced
release
of
protein-bound Fe from muscle or trace Fe
contamination from salt or processing water,
consistent with reports that ionic strength and
competing cations can modify metal binding and
mobility in complex matrices (Abdelshafy et al.,
2024). Corn-steep liquor, rich in lactic acid and
fermentative microflora, led to moderate but
consistent reductions in all understudied metals.
Lactic acid bacteria (LAB) are known to bind and
bioaccumulate a broad range of metals
(including Cd, Pb, As, Cu, Fe, and Zn) via
cell-wall biosorption and intracellular uptake,

